From: GS2107 <gs2107@local>
Subject: [PATCH] econet-eth: PPE hardware flow offload (IPv4)

- On EN7528 the PPE is set up at probe (load parameter ppe=0 leaves it alone): a 16K x 80-byte FOE table in coherent DMA memory and the engine registers with the values and order of the vendor hw_nat.ko (recovered by disassembly), except that keepalive stays off as in mainline mtk_ppe (the stack would forward the copies a second time), only IPv4 learning is enabled (the driver binds nothing else), the default CPU port stays QDMA0 and the per-VLAN-layer MTU and FE EtherType tables keep their reset values. GDM1 then forwards every received frame through the engine and parses the MediaTek tag of received frames (stag_en, set with the DMA tag parsing), without which the engine cannot find the IP header; frames of flows that are not bound still reach the CPU, and the GDM now drops frames with bad TCP/UDP checksums.
- nf_flowtable hardware offload (TC_SETUP_FT on GDM1, which DSA user ports delegate to): IPv4 TCP/UDP flows between MediaTek-tagged switch ports become HNAPT entries that leave through GDM1 directly, the switch port carried in the tag field (mtk_foe_entry_set_dsa()). A flow is bound when the engine reports its unbound entry past the bind rate (CPU reason 15 and entry index in the RX descriptor) on a plain IPv4 frame: the tuple read back from that entry selects the flow, so the hardware hash is never computed in software; flows with 802.1Q ingress are refused, and PPPoE ingress, which nf_flowtable does not describe to the driver, stays in software. The engine writes the TOS field of the entry into every packet it forwards, so the entry gets the TOS of the packet that asked for the binding, and a packet marked CE does not bind (the mark would stick to the whole flow). What the triggering packet carries becomes the flow's: per-packet DSCP collapses to one value, and CE marks added upstream after the binding are lost; the vendor driver writes the TOS the same way. Stats report the last use from the entry timestamp; a flow whose entry is no longer bound is rebound on the next request. Entries are written with the entry cache off and cleared, in the order of the vendor hwnat_delete_foe_entry; a clear that does not complete leaves the cache off. The flows offloaded through a flow block are released with the block, because nf_flowtable unbinds the block before it tears its flows down and those DESTROY commands never reach the driver. NETIF_F_HW_TC is fixed on. On GS2107 routed TCP (LAN to WAN through NAT, 1 Gb/s links) went from 656-758 Mb/s with two CPUs saturated in softirq to 934-941 Mb/s with the CPUs about 1% busy.
- RX: frames that come through the engine report source ports other than GDM1 (9, 11 and 12 seen); they belong to GDM1 like everything but GDM2. They used to log one line each, which at console loglevel 7 on the 115200-baud serial console held every such frame for about 5.5 ms.
- debugfs econet_eth/ppe (engine registers, block, flow and bind counters, entry counts per state and packet type, raw words of the first bound and other valid entries) and econet_eth/crsn (received frames per CPU reason); econet_eth/fe_regs returns 0 for an empty transfer.

